                    "profile": {
                        "address": "Federal Drive, Colorado Springs, El Paso County, Colorado, 80921",
                        "reading": "On Federal Drive in the Colorado Springs area of El Paso County, Colorado, Walmart operates an enterprise data center that the company built and brought into service with unusually little public fanfare. Site work and construction in the area were reported locally years before Walmart's involvement was confirmed, and permitting and construction filings did not initially identify the retailer as the entity behind the project, which for a time fueled speculation in the Colorado Springs business community about who was building a large, unmarked technical facility on the city's north side. The Colorado Springs Gazette and the trade publication Data Center Dynamics both eventually reported that Walmart was the owner, with Data Center Dynamics noting the company brought the site online quietly rather than through a formal announcement or ribbon cutting, a pattern consistent with how Walmart has handled several of its other internal data center projects around the country.\n\nPublic reporting has put the construction investment at roughly 100 million dollars, though no source has published the facility's critical IT capacity in megawatts, its total square footage, or a tenant list, because it is not a leased or colocation building. Walmart operates the site for its own internal use, folding it into the backbone of technology infrastructure that keeps the company's e-commerce platform, supply chain and inventory systems, and in-store point of sale and logistics technology running across its stores and distribution network. As the largest retailer in the world by revenue, Walmart has built out a substantial private data center footprint over the past decade to support that scale of operation, and the El Paso County site fits that broader pattern of enterprise capacity rather than public cloud or wholesale colocation space.\n\nColorado Springs, with its relatively low natural disaster risk, access to Colorado's power grid, and proximity to the Front Range's fiber routes, has attracted a mix of enterprise and government computing facilities over the years, and Walmart's decision to locate here follows that same logic even though the company has said little publicly about why it chose the site. Because the facility serves Walmart's own operations rather than outside customers, the company has no commercial incentive to publish capacity, uptime, or sustainability figures the way a colocation provider courting tenants would, and as of this writing none of that detail is publicly available.",
                        "sources": [
                            {
                                "url": "https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/reverse?lat=38.9857326&lon=-104.787263&format=jsonv2",
