    "profile": {
        "capacity_note": "not published beyond construction cost",
        "facility_note": "~$100M investment, El Paso County/Colorado Springs CO area. Initially undisclosed project, later confirmed as Walmart; DCD reported it came online quietly.",
        "research_status": "complete"
    },
    "reading": "Walmart is the largest retailer in the world by revenue and a New York Stock Exchange listed company that files annual reports with the United States Securities and Exchange Commission. It sits in this registry as an enterprise operator, meaning it builds and runs computing capacity for itself and sells none of it. The registry holds one United States row for the company, an enterprise data centre on the north side of Colorado Springs in El Paso County, Colorado.\n\nThe clearest public record of that building is an economic development record rather than a technical one, which is typical for enterprise projects that never market space. Local officials approved a tax incentive package worth 4.5 million dollars in May 2011, at a point when the company behind the project had not been publicly identified. Walmart bought a 24 acre parcel in September 2011, and the 210,000 square foot building came online in October 2012 at a reported construction cost of about 100 million dollars. Officials projected roughly 488 million dollars of economic impact over fifteen years. Neither the company nor the city marked the opening with an announcement or a ribbon cutting, which is why local coverage of an unidentified developer building a large unmarked technical facility ran ahead of any confirmation, and why the region treated the project as a significant win without ever holding a public event for it. That sequence, an incentive vote before the applicant is named, is common enough in data centre siting that it is worth recognising as a pattern rather than as secrecy peculiar to one company.\n\nThe power record is empty. No IT load, no utility service size, no standby generator rating and no on site generation figure has been published for this building by the company, by the city, by the county or in any filing. The construction cost and the 210,000 square foot building area are the only physical numbers on the record, and the square footage is gross building area rather than white space. Dividing cost by area, or applying a watts per square foot assumption to a gross figure, produces a capacity number that no source ever stated, and this registry does not carry one for that reason.\n\nThe site's geography is worth reading correctly. The stored street value on this row is a reverse geocode to Federal Drive, a nearest road answer rather than a verified postal address. The stored coordinates place the building roughly a fifth of a mile from the separately recorded SAP site, which sits on T5 Data Centers' 64 acre restricted use campus nearby. The two are entirely distinct projects on distinct parcels, Walmart's 24 acres bought in 2011 and SAP America's purchase of roughly 9 acres from T5 in 2016, and their proximity reflects the clustering of large computing buildings along the Federal Drive and InterQuest corridor. That corridor draws them for the same reasons in each case, comparatively cheap utility power, dry high altitude air that permits free cooling for most of the year, and low exposure to natural hazards. Finally, a facility count of one describes what this registry holds in the United States for Walmart and is not a statement about the company's total computing footprint, which Walmart does not enumerate publicly.",
